{
  "title": "My days",
  "title_target": "ჩემი დღე — Chemi dghe",
  "goal": "Talk about your daily routine with verbs that change by subject — and discover that the whole system needs only two markers: ვ- at the front and -ს at the end.",
  "bekal": [
    "form a verb for all six subjects",
    "describe your routine from morning to night",
    "say how often: always, often, sometimes, never",
    "ask about other people's activities",
    "use the object form ending in -ს"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "დიალოგი — A morning that's too early",
      "intro": "Ani and Nino have breakfast together and compare their daily habits. Almost every sentence in this dialogue is a verb — watch the prefixes and endings first, not the meanings.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "ნინო:",
          "text": "[როდის|Ro-dis] [დგები|dge-bi] [დილით|di-lit]?",
          "id": "What time do you get up in the morning?",
          "literal": "when — you-get-up — morning-in?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[შვიდ|Shvid] [საათზე|sa-at-ze] [ვდგები|vdge-bi]. [მერე|Me-re] [ვსაუზმობ|vsa-uz-mob].",
          "id": "I get up at seven. Then I have breakfast.",
          "literal": "seven — hour-at — I-get-up. then — I-breakfast."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ნინო:",
          "text": "[რას|Ras] [ჭამ|ch'am] [დილით|di-lit]?",
          "id": "What do you eat for breakfast?",
          "literal": "what-obj — you-eat — morning-in?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[პურს|P'urs] [და|da] [ყველს|q'vels]. [ყავას|Q'a-vas] [ვსვამ|vsvam], [ყოველთვის|q'o-vel-tvis].",
          "id": "Bread and cheese. I drink coffee, always.",
          "literal": "bread-obj — and — cheese-obj. coffee-obj — I-drink, always."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ნინო:",
          "text": "[მერე|Me-re] [სად|sad] [მიდიხარ|mi-di-khar]?",
          "id": "Then where do you go?",
          "literal": "then — where — you-go?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[უნივერსიტეტში|U-ni-ver-si-t'et'-shi] [მივდივარ|miv-di-var]. [იქ|Ik] [ქართულს|kar-tuls] [ვსწავლობ|vsts'av-lob].",
          "id": "I go to the university. I study Georgian there.",
          "literal": "university-in — I-go. there — Georgian-obj — I-study."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ნინო:",
          "text": "[საღამოს|Sa-gha-mos] [რას|ras] [აკეთებ|a-k'e-teb]?",
          "id": "What do you do in the evening?",
          "literal": "evening-in — what-obj — you-do?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[ხანდახან|Khan-da-khan] [მეგობრებთან|me-gob-reb-tan] [ვხვდები|vkhvde-bi], [ხანდახან|khan-da-khan] [სახლში|sakhl-shi] [ვისვენებ|vis-ve-neb].",
          "id": "Sometimes I meet friends, sometimes I rest at home.",
          "literal": "sometimes — friends-at — I-meet, sometimes — home-in — I-rest."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ნინო:",
          "text": "[ტელევიზორს|T'e-le-vi-zors] [უყურებ|u-q'u-reb]?",
          "id": "Do you watch TV?",
          "literal": "television-obj — you-watch?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[იშვიათად|Ish-vi-a-tad]. [წიგნებს|Ts'ig-nebs] [ვკითხულობ|vk'i-tkhu-lob] [და|da] [ქართულს|kar-tuls] [ვმეორებ|vme-o-reb].",
          "id": "Rarely. I read books and review my Georgian.",
          "literal": "rarely. books-obj — I-read — and — Georgian-obj — I-review."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ნინო:",
          "text": "[ყოჩაღ|Q'o-chagh]! [მე|Me] [არასდროს|a-ras-dros] [ვმეორებ|vme-o-reb] [ინგლისურს|ing-li-surs].",
          "id": "Well done! I never review my English.",
          "literal": "well-done! I — never — I-review — English-obj."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "ანი:",
          "text": "[ამიტომ|A-mi-t'om] [მე|me] [ქართულს|kar-tuls] [ვსწავლობ|vsts'av-lob], [შენ|shen] [კი|k'i] [არა|a-ra]!",
          "id": "That's why I'm the one learning Georgian, not you!",
          "literal": "that's-why — I — Georgian-obj — I-study, you — though — not!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "**Two markers explain almost every Georgian verb at A1: ვ- at the front for \"I\", -ს at the end for \"he/she\".** ვსწავლობ (I study) → სწავლობ (you study) → სწავლობ**ს** (he/she studies). \"You\" is the barest form — no prefix, no ending. The three plural forms just add **-თ**.",
        "**რას აკეთებ? = \"what are you doing?\"** You'll hear and use this sentence every day. Notice რა**ს** — the object form of რა.",
        "**The thing acted on takes -ს.** ყავა → ყავა**ს** ვსვამ (I drink coffee). წიგნი → წიგნ**ს** ვკითხულობ (I read a book). ⚠️ A typical trap for English speakers, since English marks the object only by word order. *ყავა ვსვამ* is still understood, but sounds like \"coffee I-drink\" with nothing linking them — the -ს ending is what ties them together.",
        "**The plural object form: -ებს.** წიგნი → წიგნ**ები** → წიგნ**ებს** ვკითხულობ (I read books). The endings simply stack; there's no new form to learn.",
        "**მივდივარ doesn't have ვ- at the front but in the middle.** Georgian verbs of motion have a direction prefix (მი- = away, მო- = toward) that stands **before** the subject marker: მი-**ვ**-დივარ (I go), მო-**ვ**-დივარ (I come). The \"you\" forms: მი**დი**ხარ, მო**დი**ხარ.",
        "**ვხვდები = I meet, and it requires the ending -თან.** *მეგობრებთან ვხვდები* — literally \"I meet at-friends\". Some Georgian verbs do require a particular ending, just as English says \"meet **with**\" or \"listen **to**\".",
        "**ამიტომ = that's why.** Its partner is **იმიტომ რომ** (because). *ქართულს ვსწავლობ, იმიტომ რომ საქართველოში ვცხოვრობ* — I'm learning Georgian because I live in Georgia."
      ],
